WhatsApp is allowing group chats to have up to 1,500 members on iPhone and Android, up from the previous limit of 1,024 participants, according to <a href="https://wabetainfo.com/whatsapp-is-rolling-out-larger-group-chats-with-1500-members/">WABetaInfo</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/05/Whatsapp-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1048579" /><br/>
The increase means group chat admins have room for another 476 people in a single conversation. The jump should make it easier for schools, clubs, and other large organizations to communicate with their members, rather than having to split them across separate chats.<br/>
<br/>
There's no setting to enable the new higher limit, as existing groups will gain the extra capacity automatically as the change reaches their accounts. The higher limit also applies to individual group chats within WhatsApp Communities, which bring related groups together under a shared hub.<br/>
<br/>
WhatsApp has yet to officially announced the change, and its <a href="https://faq.whatsapp.com/3242937609289432/">Help Center</a> still lists the maximum group size as 1,024 members. Even so, the rollout is already reaching some users running the latest versions of WhatsApp for iOS and Android. <br/>
<br/>
